      "updated": 1768244598315,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "🔑 Core idea\n\nEvent API returns an event page, it includes a tickets[] array. That array is derived from the\nseat map and inventory model, not a separate unrelated list. \n\nThe seat map describes all possible seats for a venue (layout, coordinates,\nsection/row/number).\n\nThe tickets[] the event API returns are the actual inventory entries for that event —\nessentially a flattened view of the seats with availability and pricing info included. \n\nSo the mapping is:\n\nseatMap (structure) + ticket inventory (tickets[]) → UI seat map with real-time availability\nand pricing\n\n\n🧠 What the Event DB tickets[] contains\n\nAccording to the Hello Interview model:\n→ tickets belongs to the Event entity\n→ It stores per-event ticket records such as:\n    → ticket ID\n    → seat details (section, row, seat number)\n    → price\n    → current status (e.g., available, sold)\n\nThis is inventory, not the seat layout itself.\nExample (conceptually):\n\n{\n  \"eventId\": \"E123\",\n  \"tickets\": [\n    {\"ticketId\": \"T1001\", \"section\": \"A\", \"row\": \"10\", \"seatNumber\": \"3\", \"price\": 120,\n\"status\":\"available\"},\n    {\"ticketId\": \"T1002\", \"section\": \"A\", \"row\": \"10\", \"seatNumber\": \"4\", \"price\": 120,\n\"status\":\"sold\"}\n  ]\n}\n\n\n🪑 How this maps to a Seat Map\n\nThe seat map (from the Venue entity) defines all possible places people can sit:\n\nVenue.seating_layout:\n  sections -> rows -> seat positions\n\nMeanwhile the tickets[] in the Event DB are generated at event creation time by\nenumerating all those seats and turning them into sellable inventory entries, e.g.:\n\nforeach seat in seating_layout\n   create ticket record with seat info + event ID\n\nSo:\n→ The seat map provides the structure and geometry (where seats are located).\n→ The event’s tickets[] provide the instances of those seats for that event with pricing and\nstatus.\n→ They’re linked by seat identifiers like (section, row, seatNumber), effectively a natural\nkey.\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 6,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "middle",
      "containerId": "duU2vzYzvS-K1ME5UKsgw",
      "originalText": "🔑 Core idea\n\nEvent API returns an event page, it includes a tickets[] array. That array is derived from the seat map and inventory model, not a separate unrelated list. \n\nThe seat map describes all possible seats for a venue (layout, coordinates, section/row/number).\n\nThe tickets[] the event API returns are the actual inventory entries for that event — essentially a flattened view of the seats with availability and pricing info included. \n\nSo the mapping is:\n\nseatMap (structure) + ticket inventory (tickets[]) → UI seat map with real-time availability and pricing\n\n\n🧠 What the Event DB tickets[] contains\n\nAccording to the Hello Interview model:\n→ tickets belongs to the Event entity\n→ It stores per-event ticket records such as:\n    → ticket ID\n    → seat details (section, row, seat number)\n    → price\n    → current status (e.g., available, sold)\n\nThis is inventory, not the seat layout itself.\nExample (conceptually):\n\n{\n  \"eventId\": \"E123\",\n  \"tickets\": [\n    {\"ticketId\": \"T1001\", \"section\": \"A\", \"row\": \"10\", \"seatNumber\": \"3\", \"price\": 120, \"status\":\"available\"},\n    {\"ticketId\": \"T1002\", \"section\": \"A\", \"row\": \"10\", \"seatNumber\": \"4\", \"price\": 120, \"status\":\"sold\"}\n  ]\n}\n\n\n🪑 How this maps to a Seat Map\n\nThe seat map (from the Venue entity) defines all possible places people can sit:\n\nVenue.seating_layout:\n  sections -> rows -> seat positions\n\nMeanwhile the tickets[] in the Event DB are generated at event creation time by enumerating all those seats and turning them into sellable inventory entries, e.g.:\n\nforeach seat in seating_layout\n   create ticket record with seat info + event ID\n\nSo:\n→ The seat map provides the structure and geometry (where seats are located).\n→ The event’s tickets[] provide the instances of those seats for that event with pricing and status.\n→ They’re linked by seat identifiers like (section, row, seatNumber), effectively a natural key.\n",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
